tgs:Interview.TGS_196_MeredithAngwin a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Is the U.S. Electric Grid Stable? Policy, Renewables, and Who Is Responsible If The Grid Fails"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct first conversation with Meredith Angwin, energy analyst and author of Shorting the Grid (already built), exploring the fraying state of the US electrical grid and the real, diffuse policy and governance decisions behind it — directly framed as the show's own real first episode specifically examining how policy and governance shape grid reliability, not just the underlying physical engineering."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Today I am joined by energy analyst Meredith Angwin to discuss the fraying state of the United States electrical grid and the policy decisions behind this unfolding situation. As a working chemist, Meredith Angwin headed projects that lowered pollution and increased reliability on the electrical grid. She's an inventor with several patents and was one of the first women to be a project manager at the Electric Power Research Institute, where she led projects in renewable and nuclear energy. Meredith also served on the coordinating committee for the consumer liaison group associated with her local grid operator, ISO New England. She continues to study and take part in grid oversight, governance, and education, and is the author of the book Shorting the Grid: The Hidden Fragility of Our Electric Grid. As many of you know, energy is a foundational topic of The Great Simplification podcast, but this is the first episode where we explore the effects of our policy and governance decisions on the reliability of the electric grids on which so many of us depend and take for granted."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-09-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-10-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.MeredithAngwin ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/196-meredith-angwin> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GzHkl3ThFak> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/TGS-197-Meredith-Angwin-Transcript-1.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Organization.ElectricPowerResearchInstitute, tgs:Work.ShortingTheGrid, tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Human.DJWhite ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Angwin directly opens with a real, structural diagnosis of grid failures generally: 'the buck doesn't stop anywhere' — using the real 2021 Texas blackout as a direct case study of diffuse accountability, where poor generation planning and inadequate winterization left no single, clear party actually responsible for the outcome. She's real and direct in distinguishing the 'physical grid' from the 'policy grid,' arguing genuine grid fragility stems as much from real, diffuse market and regulatory structures as from any single technical shortfall. A real, direct historical throughline grounds her own institutional critique: most of the US grid was originally built by vertically-integrated, regulated utilities under real, direct rate-of-return regulation, a structure now increasingly displaced by deregulated, market-based systems whose actual reliability incentives she treats as genuinely unproven at scale. She's real and direct about nuclear safety specifically, citing zero direct deaths at both Three Mile Island and Fukushima as real, concrete evidence against popular perception of nuclear risk, while directly contrasting France's real, continued nuclear expansion against Germany's real, complete nuclear phase-out as two live, divergent national bets. A real, direct personal note closes the conversation: Angwin states plainly she's grateful for the genuinely better life she's led compared to her own grandparents, arguing directly that wanting to preserve that standard of living for the next generation isn't something to be ashamed of, a real, deliberate note of gratitude rather than guilt closing an otherwise technical conversation."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Hyman G. Rickover (real, directly cited, 'father of the nuclear navy,' thorium/molten salt reactor context, not built); this episode's own citation list is otherwise extraordinarily dense with real, technical grid-policy and nuclear-engineering material (ERCOT, MISO, common-mode failure, inverter technology) rather than pointing to further individuals — DJ White is already built and directly cross-referenced via the Reality Blind 'you are baseload' citation, not a new find."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_197_OveHoeghGuldberg a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Will Coral Reefs Be Gone by 2050? How Bleaching, Acidification, and Ocean Heating are Killing Coral Reefs"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first conversation with marine biologist Ove Hoegh-Guldberg, timed to the 25th anniversary — halfway point — of his own real, landmark 1999 paper predicting global coral reef loss by 2050. Hoegh-Guldberg opens with real, direct candor about that original prediction's reception: 'many scientists wouldn't really support the conclusions at first because it just seemed incredible,' and his own real, memorable framing of the stakes — if he turned out wrong, 'that would be the sweetest egg I'd ever had.' A real, striking acceleration anchors the update: bleaching events once occurred every five to seven years, allowing recovery time; the past decade has seen 'back to back bleaching' with no recovery window at all, corals now dying faster than they can be replaced."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-08-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-10-15"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.OveHoeghGuldberg ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/197-ove-hoegh-guldberg> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8DKxpUFYmnA> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/TGS-197-Ove-Hoegh-Guldberg-Transcript.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_17_MovingFromApathyToAction ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, striking figures anchor the ecological stakes directly: roughly 25% of all oceanic biodiversity lives in or around coral reefs, and over 500 million people depend directly on reef ecosystems for food security and livelihoods. Hoegh-Guldberg is real and direct that his own research focus has shifted over the decades from pure science toward communication and policy engagement, spending substantial time with politicians directly — describing a real, current moment of political alignment in Australia across Labor, a reforming Liberal party, and the Greens as a genuine, rare opportunity worth acting on quickly. A real, unscripted moment of political candor surfaces near the close: Hoegh-Guldberg remarks that 'the Americans don't' share that same alignment, prompting Nate's own real, immediate apology — which Hoegh-Guldberg directly waves off, careful to distinguish the country from its current leadership, 'you're now being tainted by a brush which not everyone owns.' Real, concrete grounds for hope close the substantive conversation: Hoegh-Guldberg estimates that 25-30% of the emissions gap between a 3°C and 1.5°C future could be closed using off-the-shelf technology already available today, including real, direct mention of floating offshore wind. Closes on real, considered resolve rather than despair: 'keep the faith and get active and let's solve this problem together.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Johan Rockström (real, directly cited multiple times, his own real TGS episode #134 confirmed but not built); Anastassia Makarieva (real TGS episode #193, biotic pump, independently reconfirmed here — already known as a deferred citation elsewhere in this graph, e.g. Craig Tindale's TGS_207); Carlos Nobre (real TGS episode #150, independently reconfirmed here — also already known as deferred, e.g. via Andrew Millison's TGS_178); Stephen Jenkinson (real TGS episode #190, directly cross-referenced alongside Francis Weller — already built — and RR-17 — also already built — as this graph's real grief-focused episodes), now built (2026-09-02)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_198_StacyMitchell a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Challenging Monopoly Power: Why Local Business is Better for People, the Planet, and Your Wallet"@en ;
    dct:created "2025-09-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-10-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.StacyMitchell ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/198-stacy-mitchell> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ohwaf1N94sM>, <https://open.spotify.com/episode/1SLnqgoUGC84mZabc2BtgI> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/TGS-198-Stacy-Mitchell-Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Stacy Mitchell (Institute for Local Self-Reliance) argues that concentrated corporate power operates as a de facto governing force — deciding which communities thrive and which don't — not through superior performance but through structural market power ('strong arming the system'). She grounds this in real, concrete supply-chain fragility examples: a single slaughterhouse failure emptying meat shelves nationwide during COVID, one baby-formula plant failure causing a national shortage. Nate connects this directly to his own economic-Superorganism framing, comparing the system to 'the Borg.' The conversation's most consequential turn links economic powerlessness directly to democratic erosion: Mitchell argues that as communities lose local control over decisions affecting their own wellbeing, the resulting powerlessness becomes 'poison in the veins of democracy,' driving people toward strongman politics and, in the most severe cases, political violence — a real, direct causal mechanism, not just correlation. The episode closes on food deserts specifically (nearly 40 million Americans lack nearby grocery access) as a concrete, human-scale entry point into the otherwise abstract monopoly-power conversation." ;
    skos:scopeNote "Previously identified as a real, valuable cross-reference during an earlier episode-minting pass (see this graph's own note on the episode that first surfaced it) but deliberately deferred rather than stubbed prematurely. Built at MJSullivan's explicit request. CORRECTED 2026-08-29: an earlier draft of this entry asserted dct:references directly from this episode to four Work individuals (Deseret farm-bailout, World Bank food security, Pew declining trust, Carbon Majors) — but this episode never actually cites any of them; that link was standing in for 'candidate WWNS pairing,' a downstream editorial judgment, not a real citation relationship the episode itself carries. MJSullivan caught this: the correct shape is Episode -> WWNSArticle -> Work, not Episode -> Work directly, matching how tgs:WWNSArticle.WWNS_1 already properly models its own real references. This episode is not currently referenced by any built WWNSArticle; the four Works above remain real, valid candidates for a future piece, tracked outside this graph until one is actually written."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_199_SheldonSolomon a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Terror Management Theory: How Existential Dread Has Shaped the World"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first conversation with Sheldon Solomon, co-developer of Terror Management Theory (TMT), tracing the theory's real, direct origin to Solomon and fellow grad-school colleagues Jeff Greenberg and Thomas Pyszczynski encountering Ernest Becker's work (already built) as young professors in the early 1980s. Solomon lays out Becker's real, foundational argument in his own vivid terms: humans are physically unimpressive animals whose real evolutionary advantages are being intensely social and abstractly intelligent — and that same intelligence produces full self-awareness of inevitable death, which, left unmanaged, would be paralyzing."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-09-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-10-29"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.SheldonSolomon ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/199-sheldon-solomon> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=J-gjxwqoeYk> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/TGS-199-Sheldon-Solomon-Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.DarkTriad ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.DenialOfDeath, tgs:Work.TheWormAtTheCore, tgs:Work.InTheWakeOf911, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_108_TheInfluenceOfPsychopaths, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad, tgs:Interview.TGS_194_LukeKemp, tgs:Human.RobinWallKimmerer ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Solomon's own real, vivid summary of unmanaged death terror closes the theoretical setup directly: without cultural defenses, humans 'would be twitching blobs of biological protoplasm, cowering under our beds, groping for sedatives the size of sport utility vehicles.' He directly explains TMT's real, core mechanism: cultural worldviews (shared beliefs about the universe's origin, proper conduct, and some form of immortality) combined with self-esteem (living up to those cultural standards) jointly buffer death anxiety — and real, empirical studies show subtle reminders of mortality measurably increase materialism, in-group favoritism, and hostility toward those with different worldviews. A real, direct connection to money closes the economic section: real studies find death reminders increase desire for wealth and willingness to damage the environment for material gain, offering a real, direct psychological mechanism underlying this graph's own recurring concerns about consumption and growth. A real, striking counter-finding closes the political-psychology section: simply reminding people of shared human experiences (rather than differences) measurably eliminates threat-induced hostility toward out-groups including Arabs and immigrants — real, empirical evidence that the same death-anxiety mechanism driving tribalism can be redirected toward solidarity with a fairly simple intervention. Solomon's own real, unhedged magic-wand answer is structural rather than psychological: 'defund and disempower the five or six people running earth' currently in positions of power — before directly qualifying himself, arguing it's simultaneously incumbent on ordinary citizens to become more existentially mature, since removing current bad actors without addressing the underlying psychological dynamic simply invites replacement by equally destructive figures. Closes with real, direct civic advocacy: citing Thomas Jefferson's own founding rationale for the University of Virginia, Solomon argues literate, educated citizens capable of respectful disagreement are TMT's own real, practical antidote at the civilizational level, given real survey data showing most Americans can't name the three branches of government."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest transcription correction: the raw transcript renders Thomas Pyszczynski's name as 'Tom Budzinski' throughout — corrected here, confirmed directly by the official show notes. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Jeff Greenberg and Thomas Pyszczynski (Solomon's own real, direct TMT co-developers, not built, given their engagement here is entirely secondhand through Solomon's own account); Robert Jay Lifton (real, directly cited psychiatrist, 'first species responsible for our own extinction,' not built); The Dawn of Everything (Graeber and Wengrow, real, directly cited book, independently already known as a deferred citation elsewhere in this graph)."@en ;
    .


